\fs96 \cf2 Debugging\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Mojo extension for Visual Studio Code enables you to use VS Code's built-in debugger with Mojo code. This page describes the features available through the VS Code Mojo extension, as well as current limitations of the Mojo debugger.\
You can install the Mojo extension from either the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=modular-mojotools.vscode-mojo"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Visual Studio Code Marketplace}} or the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://open-vsx.org/extension/modular-mojotools/vscode-mojo"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Open VSX Registry}}. See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/install/#add-the-vs-code-extension"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Add the VS Code extension}} for more information.\
To use the Mojo extension, you must also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/install/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 install the 
\fs28 \cb12 mojo
\fs32 \cb1  package}}\'97or, if you're developing for the MAX framework, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/max/packages/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 install the 
\fs28 \cb12 modular
\fs32 \cb1  package}}, which includes the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  package.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 The Mojo extension relies on the Python extension for locating your Python environment. In some cases, this appears to default to your globally-installed environment, even when a virtual environment exists. If the Mojo extension cannot find your SDK installation, try invoking the "Python: Set Project Environment" command and selecting your virtual environment.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 For complete coverage of VS Code's debugging features, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/debugging"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Debugging in Visual Studio Code}}.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  package includes the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lldb.llvm.org/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 LLDB debugger}} and a Mojo LLDB plugin. Together these provide the low-level debugging interface for the Mojo extension. You can also use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo debug
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  command to start a command-line debugging session using LLDB or to launch a Mojo debugging session in VS Code.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  package also includes support for debugging Mojo programs running on GPU. This requires some extra software and configuration. Currently GPU debugging only works with NVIDIA GPUs. For details, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/tools/gpu-debugging/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 GPU debugging}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Start debugging\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 There are several ways to start a debug session in VS Code.\
To start debugging, you'll need to have a Mojo project to debug. There are a number of examples ranging from simple to complex in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular/tree/modular/v26.2/mojo/examples"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 our GitHub repo}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 VS Code veteran?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb13 If you're already familiar with debugging in VS Code, the material in this section will mostly be review. You might want to skip ahead to \ul Launch configurations\ulnone  or see \ul Using the debugger\ulnone  for notes on the features supported in the Mojo debugger.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Quick run or debug\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If your active editor tab contains a Mojo file with an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  entry point, one of the quickest ways to run or debug it is using the Run or Debug button in the Editor toolbar.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic unknown.png \width880 \height640 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
To start debugging the current file:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Open the Run or Debug dropdown menu and choose Debug Mojo File or Debug Mojo File in Dedicated Terminal.\uc0\u8232 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The two debug configurations differ in how they handle input and output:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Debug Mojo File launches the Mojo program detached from any terminal. Standard output and standard error output for the program are displayed in the Debug Console. You can't write to the program's standard input, but you can see the program's output and interact with the debugger in a single location.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Debug Mojo File in Dedicated Terminal creates a new instance of VS Code's integrated terminal and attaches the program's input and output to the terminal. This lets you interact with the program's standard input, standard output and standard error output in the terminal, while the Debug Console is used only for interactions with the debugger.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The Run or Debug button uses predefined launch configurations. There's currently no way to modify the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 args
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 env
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cwd
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or other settings for programs launched with the Run or Debug configurations. If you need to customize any of these things, see \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Edit launch configurations\cf2 \ulnone .\
After you choose one of the debug configurations, the button updates to show the debug symbol. Click the button to re-run the previous configuration.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 .\
