\fs48 \cf2 Other ways to start a debug session\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 There are a number of other ways to start a debug session.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Launching from the Command Palette\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you have a Mojo file open in your active editor, you can also start a debug session from the Command Palette.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Click View > Command Palette or press 
\fs28 Control+Shift+P
\fs32  (
\fs28 Command+Shift+P
\fs32  on macOS).\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Enter "Mojo" at the prompt to bring up the Mojo commands. You should see the same debug configurations described in \cf5 \ul Quick run or debug\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Launch from the File Explorer\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To launch a debug session from the File Explorer view:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Right-click on a Mojo file.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Select a Mojo debug configuration.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You should see the same debug configurations described in \cf5 \ul Quick run or debug\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Debug with F5\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Press F5 to start a debug session using the current debug configuration.\
If you don't have any existing debug configurations available to select, and your active editor contains a Mojo file with an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  entry point, pressing F5 will launch and debug the current file using the Debug Mojo File action described in \cf5 \ul Quick run or debug\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Starting the debugger from the command line\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo debug
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  command to start a debug session from the command line. You can choose from two debugging interfaces:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
With the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 --vscode
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  flag, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo debug
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  starts a debug session on VS Code if it's running and the Mojo extension is enabled.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Without the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 --vscode
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  flag, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo debug
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  starts a command-line {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lldb.llvm.org/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul LLDB debugger}} session.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can choose to build and debug a Mojo file, run and debug a compiled binary, or to attach the debugger to a running process.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 Environment variables\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb13 When you debug a program from the command line using 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb14 --vscode
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 , the program runs with the environment variables set in the terminal. When launching from inside VS Code via the GUI, the environment is defined by the VS Code \ul launch configuration\ulnone .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 For a full list of command-line options, see the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/cli/debug/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul mojo debug
\fs32 \cb1  reference page}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Start a debug session from the command line\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 With VS Code open, run the following command (either from VS Code's integrated terminal or an external shell):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb16 mojo debug \cf17 --vscode\cf15  myproject.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Or to debug a compiled binary:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb16 mojo debug \cf17 --vscode\cf15  myproject\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For best results, build with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -O0 -g
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  command-line options when you build a binary that you intend to debug\'97this produces a binary with full debug info. (When you call 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo debug
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  on a Mojo source file, it includes debug information by default.) See the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/cli/build/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul mojo build
\fs32 \cb1  reference page}} for details on compilation options.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Attach the debugger to a running process from the command line\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can also attach the debugger to a running process by specifying either the process ID or process name on the command line:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb16 mojo debug \cf17 --vscode\cf15  \cf17 --pid\cf15  \cf5 <\cf15 PROCESS_ID\cf5 >\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Or:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb16 mojo debug \cf17 --vscode\cf15  --process-name \cf5 <\cf15 PROCESS_NAME\cf5 >\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Launch configurations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 VS Code launch configurations let you define setup information for debugging your applications.\
The Mojo debugger provides the following launch configuration templates:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Debug current Mojo file. Launches and debugs the Mojo file in the active editor tab. Effectively the same as the Debug Mojo File action described in \cf5 \ul Quick run or debug\cf2 \ulnone , but with more configuration options.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Debug Mojo file. Like the previous entry, except that it identifies a specific file to launch and debug, no matter what file is displayed in the active editor.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Debug binary. This configuration operates on a prebuilt binary, which could be written in any mixture of languages supported by LLDB (Mojo, C, C++, etc.). You need to set the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 program
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  field to the path of your binary.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Attach to process. Launches a debug session attached to a running process. On launch, you choose the process you want to debug from a list of running processes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can edit any of these templates to customize them. All VS Code launch configurations must contain the following attributes:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The name of the launch configuration, which shows up in the UI (for example, "Run current Mojo file").\
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
request
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Can be either 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 launch
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (to run a program from VS Code) or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 attach
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (to attach to and debug a running file).\
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo-lldb
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for the Mojo debugger. Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo-cuda-gdb
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to debug on GPU.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In addition, Mojo launch configurations can contain the following attributes:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
args
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Any command-line arguments to be passed to the program.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
cwd
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The current working directory to run the program in.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
description
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . A longer description of the configuration, not shown in the UI.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
env
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Environment variables to be set before running the program.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
mojoFile
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Path to a Mojo file to launch and debug.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
pid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Process ID of the running process to attach to.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
program
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Path to a compiled binary to launch and debug, or the program to attach to.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
runInTerminal
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . True to run the program with a dedicated terminal, which allows the program to receive standard input from the terminal. False to run the program with its output directed to the Debug Console.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo GPU launch configurations can contain the following attributes:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
breakOnLaunch
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Set to true to automatically break when a GPU kernel launches.\
\ls12\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
initCommands
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . An array of commands to issue to the debugger on startup. To use the classic CUDA-GDB debugger backend, add the following lines to your configuration:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 "initCommands":\cf15  \cf5 [\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf15 \cb16 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf20 "set environment CUDBG_USE_LEGACY_DEBUGGER=1"\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf5 \cb16 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
],\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf15 \cb16 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf18 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf19 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf15 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
legacyDebugger
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Set to true to use the classic debugger backend.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If configuration is a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 launch
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  request, the configuration must include either the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojoFile
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 program
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  attribute.\
For 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 attach
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  requests, the configuration must include either the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 pid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 program
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  attribute.\
VS Code performs variable substitution on the launch configurations. You can use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 $\{workspaceFolder\}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to substitute the path to the current workspace, and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 $\{file\}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to represent the file in the active editor tab. For a complete list of variables, see the VS Code {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/variables-reference"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Variables reference}}.\
For more information, see the VS Code documentation for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/debugging#_launch-configurations"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Launch configurations}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 Compilation options\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb13 Mojo launch configurations don't allow you to specify compilation options. If you need to specify compilation options, you can build the binary using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/cli/build/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb14 \ul mojo build}}, then use a launch configuration with the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb14 program
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  option to launch the compiled binary. Or if you \ul start the debugger from the command line\ulnone , you can pass compilation options to the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb14 mojo debug
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  command.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Edit launch configurations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To edit launch configurations:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If the Run and Debug view isn't already open, click the Run and Debug icon in the Activity Bar (on the left side of the VS Code window) or press 
\fs28 Control+Shift+D
\fs32  (
\fs28 Command+Shift+D
\fs32  on macOS).\uc0\u8232 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Create or open the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 launch.json
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	i	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If you see the Run start view, click create a launch.json file.\
\ls13\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	ii	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If you already have launch configurations set up, click the gear icon next to the Launch configurations menu. {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Select Mojo from the list of debuggers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 VS Code opens the new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 launch.json
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file in an editor tab, with templates for some common debug actions. Click Add configuration to add a new configuration template.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Using the debugger\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When a debug session is running, use the debug toolbar to pause, continue, and step through the program.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
The buttons on the toolbar are:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Continue/Pause: If the program is stopped, resume the normal execution of the program up to the next breakpoint, signal or crash. Otherwise, pause all the threads of the program at once.\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Step Over: Execute the next line of code without stopping at function calls.\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Step Into: Execute the next line of code and stop at the first function call. If the program is stopped just before a function call, steps into the function so you can step through it line-by-line.\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Step Out: Finish the execution of the current function and stop right after returning to the parent function.\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Restart: If this is a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 launch
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  session, terminate the current program and restart the debug session. Otherwise, detach from the target process and reattach to it.\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Stop: If this is a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 launch
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  session, terminate the current program. Otherwise, detach from the target process without killing it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The debugger currently has the following limitations:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls15\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
No support for breaking automatically on Mojo errors.\
\ls15\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When stepping out of a function, the returned value is not displayed.\
\ls15\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
LLDB doesn't support stopping or resuming individual threads.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Breakpoints\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Mojo debugger supports setting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/debugging#_breakpoints"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul standard breakpoints}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/debugging#_logpoints"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul logpoints}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/debugging#_function-breakpoints"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul function breakpoints}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/debugging#_data-breakpoints"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul data breakpoints}}, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/debugging#_triggered-breakpoints"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul triggered breakpoints}}, as described in the VS Code documentation. The Mojo debugger also supports error breakpoints (also known as "break on raise"), which break whenever a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raise
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement is executed.\
When debugging Mojo code, the debugger doesn't support conditional breakpoints based on an expression (it does support hit counts, which VS Code classifies as a kind of conditional breakpoint).\
When editing a breakpoint, you're offered four options:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls16\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Expression. Set a conditional breakpoint (not currently supported).\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Hit Count. Add a hit count to a breakpoint (supported).\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Log Message. Add a logpoint (supported)\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Wait for Breakpoint. Add a triggered breakpoint (supported).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Set a hit count breakpoint\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A hit count breakpoint is a breakpoint that only breaks execution after the debugger hits it a specified number of times.\
To add a hit count breakpoint:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls17\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Right click in the left gutter of the editor where you want to place the breakpoint, and select Add Conditional Breakpoint.\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Select Hit Count from the menu and enter the desired hit count.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To change an existing breakpoint to a hit count breakpoint:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Right click on the breakpoint in the left gutter of the editor and select Edit breakpoint.\
\ls18\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Select Hit Count from the menu and enter the desired hit count.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also edit a breakpoint from the Breakpoints section of the Run and Debug view:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls19\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Right-click on the breakpoint and select Edit Condition, or,\
\ls19\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Click the Edit Condition icon next to the breakpoint.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This brings up the same menu, next to the breakpoint in the editor tab.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Enable error breakpoints\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can enable and disable error breakpoints in VS Code by selecting "Mojo Raise" in the Breakpoints section of the Run and Debug view. If enabled during debugging, executing a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raise
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement causes the debugger to stop execution and highlight the line of code where the error was raised.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 View local variables\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When a program is paused in the debugger, the editor shows local variable values inline. You can also find them in the Variables section of the Run and Debug view.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 4. Local variable values displayed in the debugger\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 View the call stack\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When a program is paused in the debugger, the Run and Debug view shows the current call stack. (You may see multiple call stacks, one for each active thread in the program.)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 5. Call stack in Run and Debug view\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Call Stack section of the Run and Debug view shows a stack frame for each function call in the current call stack. Clicking on the name of the function highlights the current line in that function. For example, in Figure 5, the program is paused at a breakpoint in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 nested2()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but the parent function, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 nested1()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is selected in the call stack. The editor highlights the current line in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 nested1()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (that is, the call to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 nested2()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) and shows the current local variable values for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 nested1()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Use the Debug Console\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Debug Console gives you a command-line interface to the debugger. The Debug Console processes LLDB commands and Mojo expressions.\
Anything prefixed with a colon (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 :
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) is treated as an LLDB command. Any other input is treated as an expression.\
Currently Mojo expressions are limited to inspecting variables and their fields. The console also supports subscript notation (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 vector[index]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) for certain data structures in the standard library, including 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
In the future, we intend to provide a way for arbitrary data structures to support subscript notation in the Debug Console.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 The Debug Console only accepts input when the program is paused.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Tips and tricks\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 There are several features in the standard library that aren't directly related to the debugger, but which can help you debug your programs. These include:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls20\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Programmatic breakpoints.\
\ls20\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Setting parameters from the Mojo command line.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Set a programmatic breakpoint\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To break at a specific point in your code, you can use the built-in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/breakpoint/breakpoint/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul breakpoint()}} function:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 if\cf15  some_value\cf5 .\cf15 is_valid\cf5 ():\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf15 \cb16    do_the_right_thing\cf5 ()\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb16 else:\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf15 \cb16    \cf6 # We should never get here!\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb16    breakpoint\cf5 ()\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If you have VS Code open and run this code in debug mode (either using VS Code or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo debug
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), hitting the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 breakpoint()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  call causes an error, which triggers the debugger.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 Assertions\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb13 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/testing/testing/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb14 \ul testing}} module includes a number of ways to specify assertions. Assertions also trigger an error, so can open the debugger in the same way that a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb14 breakpoint()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  call will.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Set parameters from the Mojo command line\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/sys/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul sys}} module to retrieve parameter values specified on the Mojo command line. Among other things, this is an easy way to switch debugging logic on and off. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 from\cf15  std\cf5 .\cf15 sys \cf5 import\cf15  is_defined\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb16 def\cf15  \cf21 some_function_with_issues\cf5 ()\cf15  raises\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf15 \cb16     \cf6 # ...\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf5 comptime\cf15  \cf5 if\cf15  is_defined\cf5 [\cf20 "DEBUG_ME"\cf5 ]():\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb16         breakpoint\cf5 ()\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To activate this code, use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/cli/debug/#compilation-options"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul -D
\fs32 \cb1  command-line option}} to define 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DEBUG_ME
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb16 mojo debug \cf17 -D\cf15  DEBUG_ME main.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is_defined()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function returns a compile-time true or false value based on whether the specified name is defined. Since the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 breakpoint()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  call is inside a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/comptime-evaluation/#comptime-if"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul comptime if
\fs32 \cb1  statement}}, it is only included in the compiled code when the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DEBUG_ME
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  name is defined on the command line.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Troubleshooting\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 error: can't connect to the RPC debug server socket
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo debug --vscode
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  gives you the message 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 error: can't connect to the RPC debug server socket: Connection refused
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , try the following possible fixes:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Make sure VS Code is open.\
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If VS Code is already open, try restarting VS Code.\
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If there are other VS Code windows open, try closing them and then restarting. This error can sometimes occur when multiple windows have opened and closed in certain orders.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 error: couldn't get a valid response from the RPC server
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo debug --vscode
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  gives you the message 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 error: couldn't get a valid response from the RPC server
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , try the following possible fixes:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls22\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Make sure VS Code is open to a valid Mojo codebase. This error can sometimes happen if the VS Code window is open to some other codebase.\
\ls22\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If there are multiple VS Code windows open, try closing all but the one you wish to debug in.\
\ls22\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Restart VS Code.\
\ls22\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Reinstall the SDK and restart VSCode.\
\ls22\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If you are working on a development version of the SDK, make sure that all SDK tools are properly built with your build system, and then reload VS Code.\
\ls22\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
As a last resort, restarting your entire computer can fix this problem.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If these steps don't help, please file an issue. We'd love your help identifying possible causes and fixes!\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri960\partightenfactor0
